var a=[{title:`Why habits matter`,text:`Strength, cardiovascular capacity and movement skill develop through repeated practice. A workout that looks great on paper is useless if it is too long, inconvenient or unpleasant to repeat. That makes frequency and adherence part of program design as well as a question of motivation.`},{title:`Link training to a cue`,text:`Tie training to something that already happens or to a fixed appointment: “After work on Monday and Thursday, I do my 25-minute session.” Get your clothes, space or equipment ready in advance. Make the first step tiny, such as putting on your shoes and starting a five-minute warm-up, so you don’t depend on enthusiasm.`},{title:`Have a minimum session`,text:`On a busy day, one squat variation, one push or pull, and a short walk may keep the habit alive. The minimum is not a permanent replacement for progressive training. Its job is to stop one missed day from turning into several missed weeks. Record what you actually did, and don’t log a shortened workout as complete.`},{title:`Handle setbacks calmly`,text:`A missed session doesn’t erase your earlier adaptations. Pick up with the next planned workout and don’t double the workload to “repay” missed training. After illness, severe fatigue or a long break, lower the intensity and volume until your usual tolerance returns.`},{title:`Check your environment`,text:`Poor sleep, a complicated trip to the gym, equipment you can’t get to and sessions that are too long can all undermine consistency. Fixing these may help more than finding a new exercise. Enjoying your training and choosing it yourself also make a routine easier to keep.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`Set a realistic number of training days and your preferred days in Profile, and enter your space and equipment so the plan picks only exercises that fit. Workout reminders on the Dashboard can be your cue. History shows this week’s workouts and your total, and progress counts across many weeks, so you don’t need a perfect streak.`}];var n=[{label:`CDC activity habits and planning`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/healthy-weight-growth/physical-activity/getting-started.html`},{label:`WHO activity recommendations`,url:`https://www.who.int/europe/news-room/fact-sheets/item/physical-activity`}];var o={sections:a,sources:n};export{o as default,a as sections,n as sources};